<article class="env-remark" id="013T">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/013T">Remark <span data-tag="013T">3.6.3.4</span>.</a> Let $f: X \rightarrow Y$ be a continuous function between topological spaces. Then $f$ is a weak homotopy equivalence if and only if it satisfies the following pair of conditions: </p>
<p><ul>
<li><p>
The induced map of path components $\pi _0(f): \pi _0(X) \rightarrow \pi _0(Y)$ is a bijection. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
For every point $x \in X$ and every $n \geq 1$, the map of homotopy groups $\pi _{n}(f): \pi _{n}( X, x) \rightarrow \pi _{n}(Y, f(x) )$ is an isomorphism. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
<p> This follows by applying Theorem <a data-tag="00WV" href="/tag/00WV">3.2.7.1</a> to the map of Kan complexes $\operatorname{Sing}_{\bullet }(f): \operatorname{Sing}_{\bullet }(X) \rightarrow \operatorname{Sing}_{\bullet }(Y)$ (see Example <a data-tag="00VR" href="/tag/00VR">3.2.2.7</a>). </p>
</article>
